    <div class="article-wrapper">
      <a href="blog" class="article-back"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to blog</a>

      <div class="article-meta">
        <span class="blog-tag">AI &amp; Automation</span>
        <span><i class="fa-regular fa-user"></i> Adam Sheridan</span>
        <span><i class="fa-regular fa-calendar"></i> April 2026</span>
        <span><i class="fa-regular fa-clock"></i> 7 min read</span>
      </div>

      <p><strong>Traditional automation</strong> follows fixed rules to complete repetitive tasks automatically. It operates on "if this happens, then do that" logic and performs the same action every time, regardless of context. <strong>AI automation</strong> uses artificial intelligence to understand context, make decisions, and handle tasks that require judgement, such as interpreting unstructured data, generating content, or classifying information that doesn't fit neatly into predefined categories.</p>

      <p>That's the short answer. But which one does your business actually need? In most cases, the answer is both, though probably not in the way you'd expect.</p>

      <h2>Traditional automation: the reliable workhorse</h2>

      <p>Traditional automation (sometimes called rule-based or workflow automation) is the kind that's been around for years. Tools like Make.com, Zapier, and Power Automate are built around this concept. You define a trigger, set up a sequence of actions, and the system executes them exactly the same way every time.</p>

      <p>Here are some typical examples:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>When a form is submitted, create a CRM contact and send a confirmation email</li>
        <li>When a deal moves to "Won" in your pipeline, generate an invoice and notify the accounts team</li>
        <li>Every Monday at 9am, pull last week's sales data and send a summary to the team</li>
        <li>When a customer's subscription is due for renewal in 30 days, trigger a reminder sequence</li>
      </ul>

      <p>These workflows are predictable, testable, and dependable. They don't get creative, they don't improvise, and they don't make judgement calls. That's precisely their strength. You know exactly what will happen because you defined every step.</p>

      <p>For a deeper look at the <a href="blog-5-automations-every-business" style="color:var(--purple-light);">essential automations every business should have</a>, we've written a separate guide.</p>

      <h2>AI automation: the intelligent layer</h2>

      <p><a href="glossary#ai-automation" style="color:var(--purple-light);">AI automation</a> adds a layer of intelligence on top of workflows. Instead of following rigid rules, it can interpret, decide, and generate. This is what makes it useful for tasks that traditional automation simply can't handle.</p>

      <p>Here are some real examples:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>A customer sends an email asking about their order status, a return policy, and whether you stock a particular product. AI reads the email, understands there are three separate questions, pulls the relevant information, and drafts a response that addresses all three.</li>
        <li>Your sales team receives 200 inbound leads per week. AI scores each one based on the language they used in their enquiry, their company size, and their likely budget, then routes high-priority leads to senior reps.</li>
        <li>You need 30 product descriptions written for your e-commerce site. AI generates first drafts based on your product specifications and brand voice, cutting days of writing down to hours of editing.</li>
        <li>Customer feedback comes in across reviews, surveys, and support tickets. AI analyses the sentiment, identifies recurring themes, and produces a weekly summary of what customers are actually saying.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The common thread? These are tasks where the input is variable, unstructured, or requires interpretation. Traditional automation can't read an email and understand what the customer is really asking. AI can.</p>

      <h2>When traditional automation is enough</h2>

      <p>Here's something that might surprise you: for most small and medium businesses, traditional automation handles 70-80% of what needs automating. The tasks that eat up the most time tend to be predictable, repetitive, and rule-based. Moving data between systems, sending scheduled emails, creating records, updating spreadsheets, triggering notifications.</p>

      <p>Traditional automation is the right choice when:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>The process follows the same steps every time</li>
        <li>The inputs and outputs are structured (form fields, database records, dates, numbers)</li>
        <li>You can define clear rules for every possible scenario</li>
        <li>Speed and reliability are more important than flexibility</li>
      </ul>

      <p>If you can write the process as a flowchart with clear yes/no decisions, traditional automation will handle it perfectly.</p>

      <h2>When you need AI automation</h2>

      <p>AI becomes necessary when the task involves:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Unstructured data.</strong> Free-text emails, PDFs, images, voice recordings, social media posts. Anything that doesn't arrive in neat, labelled fields.</li>
        <li><strong>Content generation.</strong> Writing emails, blog posts, product descriptions, social media content, or personalised outreach at scale.</li>
        <li><strong>Classification and routing.</strong> Deciding which category something belongs in, which team should handle it, or how urgent it is, based on context rather than simple keywords.</li>
        <li><strong>Summarisation and analysis.</strong> Condensing long documents, extracting key points from meeting transcripts, or identifying patterns in customer feedback.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>If you find yourself saying "a person needs to read this and decide what to do," that's often a task where AI can help.</p>

      <div class="article-callout">
        <h4>A practical test</h4>
        <p>Ask yourself: "Could I write a complete set of if/then rules for this task?" If yes, use traditional automation. If the answer is "it depends" or "someone needs to use their judgement," that's where AI adds value.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>The hybrid approach: what most businesses actually need</h2>

      <p>In practice, the most effective automation setups combine both types. Traditional automation handles the structured workflow, and AI steps in for the specific moments that require intelligence.</p>

      <p>Here's what a hybrid workflow looks like in a real business:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Traditional:</strong> A new enquiry arrives via your website form. The system creates a CRM record and sends an acknowledgement email.</li>
        <li><strong>AI:</strong> The system reads the enquiry message, categorises it (sales, support, partnership, spam), and assesses the lead quality based on the language and details provided.</li>
        <li><strong>Traditional:</strong> Based on the AI's classification, the lead is routed to the right team member and assigned a priority level. A follow-up task is created.</li>
        <li><strong>AI:</strong> A personalised follow-up email is drafted based on the specific details the prospect mentioned in their enquiry.</li>
        <li><strong>Traditional:</strong> The drafted email is queued for review and sent at the optimal time.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Notice how AI handles the bits that require interpretation (reading the message, assessing quality, writing a personalised response), while traditional automation handles everything else (creating records, routing, scheduling, sending). This is the pattern that works best for most businesses.</p>

      <h2>Cost comparison</h2>

      <p>Traditional automation is generally cheaper to build and run. Tools like Make.com cost as little as 7-20 GBP per month. The workflows are quicker to set up and don't incur per-use AI costs.</p>

      <p>AI automation adds cost in two ways: the AI processing fees (typically charged per API call or per token) and the additional complexity in building and testing. A workflow that includes AI steps might cost 2-3 times more to build initially and 10-50 GBP per month more to run, depending on volume.</p>

      <p>The key question isn't "which is cheaper?" but "which delivers more value?" If AI automation saves a team member two hours a day on email triage, the 30 GBP monthly API cost is trivial compared to the time saved.</p>

      <p>Our <a href="../services/ai-automation" style="color:var(--purple-light);">AI and automation services</a> page covers what's involved in building these systems if you'd like to explore further.</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>The smartest businesses don't choose between AI and traditional automation. They use traditional automation as the backbone and add AI only where it genuinely solves a problem that rules can't.</p>
      </blockquote>

